    "df.available_disk": {
      "title": "available_disk",
      "body": "What is this?\nBytes available for new writes on the root filesystem.\n\nWhy it matters\nThis is the operational free space number. It is the one that prevents log loss and failed deploys.\n\nHow to read\n- Keep a healthy buffer, not just a few megabytes.\n- Fast downward trends matter more than a single snapshot.\n- If it keeps falling, find the writer before the filesystem fills.\n\nNext check\nCompare with root_use_pct and file descriptor usage."
    },
    "df.filesystems": {
      "title": "filesystems",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for df.filesystems.\\n\\nType\\n- Table\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "df.root_use_pct": {
      "title": "root_use_pct",
      "body": "What is this?\nPercentage of the root filesystem that is in use.\n\nWhy it matters\nThis is the easiest disk-capacity alarm to understand. Once the root filesystem fills, many services fail in ugly ways.\n\nHow to read\n- Watch the trend, not just the absolute number.\n- 80% can already be uncomfortable on busy hosts.\n- 90%+ is usually a cleanup or expansion task.\n\nNext check\nCompare with available_disk and diskstats.active_devices."
    },
    "df.total_disk": {
      "title": "total_disk",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for df.total_disk.\\n\\nType\\n- Bytes\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "df.used_disk": {
      "title": "used_disk",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for df.used_disk.\\n\\nType\\n- Bytes\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "diskstats.active_devices": {
      "title": "active_devices",
      "body": "What is this?\nThe number of block devices that have seen real I/O activity since boot.\n\nWhy it matters\nIt is a quick way to tell whether storage load is concentrated on a few devices or spread across many.\n\nHow to read\n- Low count with high I/O pressure points to a few hot disks.\n- High count with low pressure can be background noise.\n- Use the device table for the real breakdown.\n\nNext check\nCompare with pressure.io_some_avg10 and root disk usage."
    },
    "diskstats.devices": {
      "title": "devices",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for diskstats.devices.\\n\\nType\\n- Table\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "diskstats.io_queue_depth_distribution": {
      "title": "IO Queue Depth Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for storage queue depth samples across time.\n\nWhy it matters\nQueue depth is a direct signal of whether the device is keeping up with demand.\n\nHow to read\n- count: observations\n- max: peak device queueing\n- buckets: whether saturation is rare or regular\n"
    },
    "diskstats.read_wait_distribution": {
      "title": "Disk Read Wait Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for disk read wait times, including min/max/count and buckets for tail latency.\n\nWhy it matters\nRead wait distributions reveal storage stalls better than raw throughput.\n\nHow to read\n- count: sample count\n- max: worst read stall\n- buckets: whether long waits are frequent\n"
    },
    "diskstats.write_wait_distribution": {
      "title": "Disk Write Wait Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for disk write wait times, especially useful when flushes or fsyncs create spikes.\n\nWhy it matters\nWrite wait tails correlate with user-visible save delays and I/O queueing.\n\nHow to read\n- count: sample volume\n- max: worst wait\n- min: baseline latency\n"
    },
