    "net/tcp.TCPBacklog_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP Backlog Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for accept backlog and related queued connection classes.\n\nWhy it matters\nA backlog that spends time near its maximum means the listener is falling behind incoming traffic.\n\nHow to read\n- count: sample count\n- max: peak backlog pressure\n- min: idle baseline\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPLatency_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP Latency Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for end-to-end TCP latency measurements with min/max/count and percentile-like buckets.\n\nWhy it matters\nLatency distribution shows user impact better than a single average.\n\nHow to read\n- count: sample volume\n- min: best case path\n- max: tail latency and stalls\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPRecvQueue_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP Receive Queue Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for receive queue depth, especially useful when traffic is bursty.\n\nWhy it matters\nPersistent receive queue growth can point to application backpressure or slow consumers.\n\nHow to read\n- count: how many observations exist\n- max: highest backlog depth\n- buckets: whether spikes are isolated or repeated\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPRetransSegs_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP Retransmissions Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for retransmission counters that should be read as a distribution: min, max, count, and related buckets.\n\nWhy it matters\nA single spike can be harmless, but repeated retransmits point to loss, congestion, or path instability.\n\nHow to read\n- count: how often retransmits happen\n- max: the worst burst or peak window\n- min: the quiet baseline\n\nCommon mistake\nChecking only the peak and missing whether the pattern is persistent.\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPRto_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP RTO Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for TCP retransmission timeout values, best read through min/max/count and bucketed ranges.\n\nWhy it matters\nA widening timeout distribution usually means the network path is getting slower or more variable.\n\nHow to read\n- count: how many timeout samples were seen\n- max: worst observed timeout\n- buckets: whether the tail is getting heavier\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPSendQueue_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP Send Queue Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for send queue occupancy, useful when the queue is usually empty but occasionally builds up sharply.\n\nWhy it matters\nA long tail in the send queue often means congestion, pacing issues, or an overloaded peer.\n\nHow to read\n- count: number of samples\n- max: worst queue buildup\n- min: baseline queue occupancy\n"
    },
    "net/tcp.TCPSynBacklog_distribution": {
      "title": "TCP SYN Backlog Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for half-open connection pressure during SYN intake.\n\nWhy it matters\nRising SYN backlog values can indicate connection floods, capacity limits, or delayed handshakes.\n\nHow to read\n- count: how many samples were recorded\n- max: worst backlog saturation\n- buckets: whether the issue is sharp or sustained\n"
    },
