    "pressure.io_some_total": {
      "title": "io_some_total",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for pressure.io_some_total.\\n\\nType\\n- Integer\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "pressure.io_stall_distribution": {
      "title": "IO Stall Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for IO stall times, often used to read contention against storage and filesystem activity.\n\nWhy it matters\nIO stall tails are the clearest user-facing signal that storage is backing up.\n\nHow to read\n- count: sample count\n- max: worst stall window\n- buckets: whether the problem is bursty or persistent\n"
    },
    "pressure.memory_full_avg10": {
      "title": "memory_full_avg10",
      "body": "What is this?\nThe fraction of the last 10 seconds where all non-idle tasks were stalled on memory.\n\nWhy it matters\nThis is severe memory pressure. It means the whole system is being held up by memory reclaim or swap.\n\nHow to read\n- Any non-zero value is serious.\n- If it persists, the machine is effectively degraded.\n- Correlate it with pswpout and pgmajfault.\n\nNext check\nLook at MemAvailable and vmstat.pswpout."
    },
    "pressure.memory_full_avg300": {
      "title": "memory_full_avg300",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for pressure.memory_full_avg300.\\n\\nType\\n- Float\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
