    "pressure.memory_stall_distribution": {
      "title": "Memory Stall Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for memory stall samples and tail behavior.\n\nWhy it matters\nMemory stalls show when tasks are blocked on allocation, reclaim, or swap-related delays.\n\nHow to read\n- count: observations\n- max: worst memory stall\n- buckets: whether delay is spreading across the workload\n"
    },
    "proc/interrupts.irq_distribution": {
      "title": "IRQ Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for hard interrupt counts and their spread across observation windows.\n\nWhy it matters\nUneven IRQ distributions can point to device hot spots, affinity problems, or noisy hardware.\n\nHow to read\n- count: total samples\n- max: peak IRQ burst\n- min: quiet baseline\n"
    },
    "proc/loadavg.runnable_distribution": {
      "title": "Runnable Load Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for runnable task pressure and load distribution around the scheduler.\n\nWhy it matters\nRunnable load that stays high means the machine is busy, not just occupied.\n\nHow to read\n- count: samples collected\n- max: worst contention window\n- buckets: whether load is spiky or sustained\n"
    },
    "proc/softirqs.softirq_distribution": {
      "title": "SoftIRQ Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for softirq activity classes, useful for seeing interrupt handling bursts.\n\nWhy it matters\nSoftirq spikes can correlate with network floods, storage completion storms, or CPU imbalance.\n\nHow to read\n- count: event volume\n- max: worst burst\n- buckets: whether activity is narrow or broad\n"
    },
    "proc/stat.context_switch_distribution": {
      "title": "Process Context Switch Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for process-level context switch spread, useful when one workload is churning too much.\n\nWhy it matters\nA heavy distribution can indicate lock contention, wakeup storms, or overly chatty polling loops.\n\nHow to read\n- count: total samples\n- max: peak switching burst\n- min: quiet floor\n"
    },
    "proc/stat.cpu_time_distribution": {
      "title": "CPU Time Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for CPU time samples and bucketed runtime classes.\n\nWhy it matters\nA stretched CPU time distribution suggests mixed workloads or uneven scheduling.\n\nHow to read\n- count: number of samples\n- max: hottest runtime slice\n- buckets: whether the workload is bimodal or smooth\n"
    },
    "processes.process_count": {
      "title": "process_count",
      "body": "What is this?\nThe total number of processes currently visible under /proc.\n\nWhy it matters\nA growing process count can signal a fork storm, container churn, or a process leak.\n\nHow to read\n- A sudden jump is worth checking immediately.\n- A slow climb over time can be a leak.\n- Pair it with context_switches if the host feels busy.\n\nNext check\nCompare with file descriptor usage and procs_running."
    },
    "processes.processes": {
      "title": "processes",
      "body": "Auto-generated first-pass article for processes.processes.\\n\\nType\\n- Table\\n\\nHow to read\\n- Track trend in Diff/Graph before deciding.\\n- Cross-check with related source-level guide and pressure/capacity metrics.\\n\\nNext step\\n- Use this as first-pass context, then drill into neighboring fields in the same source."
    },
    "schedstat.context_switch_distribution": {
      "title": "Context Switch Distribution",
      "body": "What is this?\nA grouped article for context switch counts and their spread over time.\n\nWhy it matters\nToo many switches can mean lock contention, short-lived work, or scheduler churn.\n\nHow to read\n- count: event volume\n- max: bursty switching peaks\n- min: quiet baseline\n"
    },
    "schedstat.cpu_count": {
